  "sampleAnswer": "Authentication verifies who the caller is; authorization verifies what that identity may do. Test missing/invalid/expired credentials, token rotation, role/permission boundaries, object-level access, privilege escalation, tenant isolation, and safe error responses.",
  "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: COMMON\n\nExperience level from source: Intermediate / QA Engineer\n\nQuestion:\nHow would you test authentication and authorization for an API?\n\nAnswer:\nAuthentication verifies who the caller is; authorization verifies what that identity may do. Test missing/invalid/expired credentials, token rotation, role/permission boundaries, object-level access, privilege escalation, tenant isolation, and safe error responses.\n\nExample:\nExample: POST /orders with a valid payload should return the documented success response; repeat the request, remove required fields, use an invalid token, and send boundary amounts to validate error and idempotency behavior.\n\nInterview Tip:\nAnswer in a structured way: clarify the requirement, identify risk, explain the test approach, describe evidence, and state how you would communicate the result. For scenario questions, prioritize rather than claiming that everything can be tested equally.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nTesting fundamentals, analytical thinking, risk assessment, defect investigation, communication, technical judgment, automation awareness, and the ability to make practical quality decisions.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n• Understand requirements and business risk before writing tests. • Cover positive, negative, boundary, integration, and failure scenarios as relevant. • Use evidence: logs, traces, screenshots, test data, environment details, and reproducible steps. • Do not hide uncertainty; document assumptions and investigate unknowns. • Automate where repetition and stability justify the maintenance cost. • For release decisions, communicate residual risk instead of giving unsupported guarantees.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n• What edge cases would you test? • How would you prioritize these tests? • How would you automate this scenario? • What evidence would you collect if the issue is intermittent? • How would you decide whether the risk is acceptable for release?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n• Listing hundreds of test cases without prioritization. • Confusing severity with priority or retesting with regression. • Treating automation as a replacement for all manual testing. • Adding retries to hide flaky tests instead of investigating them. • Making release decisions without considering business impact and residual risk. ------------------------------------------------------------------------",
  "keyPoints": "• Understand requirements and business risk before writing tests. • Cover positive, negative, boundary, integration, and failure scenarios as relevant. • Use evidence: logs, traces, screenshots, test data, environment details, and reproducible steps. • Do not hide uncertainty; document assumptions and investigate unknowns. • Automate where repetition and stability justify the maintenance cost. • For release decisions, communicate residual risk instead of giving unsupported guarantees.",
  "commonMistakes": "• Listing hundreds of test cases without prioritization. • Confusing severity with priority or retesting with regression. • Treating automation as a replacement for all manual testing. • Adding retries to hide flaky tests instead of investigating them. • Making release decisions without considering business impact and residual risk. ------------------------------------------------------------------------",
